This example shows HLS_TOP_AUTORESTART with an infinite (-1) launch count and
hls::stream I/O in Vitis HLS.

The kernel is launched once with launch(-1, ...). It never returns and never
completes, so is_done() and wait_for_done() are not legal. Its scalar input c is
stable, and every result comes back over the hls::stream output, which the
testbench drains with blocking reads for the exact number of expected results.

Files Included in this Package
==============================
test.h          HLS_TOP_AUTORESTART declaration and shared constants
test.cpp        Design (synthesized)
test_tb.cpp     Testbench
hls_config.cfg  Vitis HLS configuration
run_hls.tcl     Tcl flow script
description.json
run.py          Optional Python flow script (vitis -s run.py)
README

Requirements
============
HLS_TOP_AUTORESTART requires a Vitis build that provides it (2026.2 or later).

Running the Design (edit run_hls.tcl to set $hls_exec and enable specific run steps)
=========================================================
vitis-run --mode hls --tcl run_hls.tcl

Running the Design using the Python Script
=========================================
vitis -s run.py
